                %a{:href => "#References"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+structure"} mathematical structure
            is
            %em essentially algebraic
            if its definition involves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+relation"} partially defined operations
            satisfying equational laws, where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain"} domain
            of any given operation is a subset where various other operations happen to be equal. An actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
            is one where all operations are total
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
          %p
            The most familiar example may be the (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+category"}> strict
            ) notion of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
            consists of a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0
            of objects, a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1
            of morphisms, source and target maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s,t : C_1 \to C_0
            and so on, but composition is only defined for pairs of morphisms where the source of one happens to equal the target of the other.
          %p
            Essentially algebraic theories can be understood through
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            at least when they are finitary, so that all operations have only finitely many arguments. This gives a generalisation of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theories
          %p
            As the domains of the operations are given by the solutions to equations, they may be understood using the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizer"} equalizer
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
            — or in other words, finite products and also equalizers (from which all other finite limits, including
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p As alluded to above, the most concise and elegant definition is through category theory. The traditional definition is this:
          .un_def
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              An
              %strong essentially algebraic theory
              or
              %strong finite limits theory
              is a category that is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+category"} finitely complete
              %strong model
              of an essentially algebraic theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: T \to Set
            %p
              that is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+exact"} left exact
              %strong homomorphism
              of models is a natural transformation
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo :
                    %mi F
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha : F \to F'
            %p
              between left exact functors
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo ,
                      %mi F
                      %mo ′
                      %mo :
                      %mi T
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F, F' : T \to Set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              and the homomorphisms between them form a category
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Mod
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi Lex
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi T
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod(T) = Lex(T, Set)
            %p
              More generally, for any category with finite limits
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              = succeed "," do
                %strong
                  models of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                  in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Lex
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi T
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lex(T, X)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: T \to X
              as objects and natural transformations between these as morphisms.
          %p
            However, the finiteness restriction on the limits above is not part of the core concept of an ‘essentially algebraic’ structure, so one may prefer to call a category with finite limits a
            %strong finitary
            essentially algebraic theory. We do this in what follows.
          %p A more traditional syntactic definition (following Adámek–Rosicky; see the references below) is as follows:
          .un_def
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              An
              %strong essentially algebraic theory
              is a quadruple
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Def
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma = (\Sigma, E, \Sigma_t, Def)
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              is a many-sorted
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signature+%28in+logic%29"} signature
              consisting only of operation symbols,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              is a set of
              = succeed "-equations," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi t
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_t \subseteq \Sigma
              is a set of operation symbols called “total”, and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Def
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Def
              is a function which assigns, to each operation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \in \Sigma - \Sigma_t
              of type
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi I
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mi i
                      %mo →
                      %mi s
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{i \in I} s_i \to s
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Def
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Def(\sigma)
              of
              = succeed "-equations" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_t
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Var
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i \in Var(s_i)
            %p
              A (set-theoretic)
              %strong model
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              of a theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              assigns to each sort
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
              a set
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M(s)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma: \prod_{i \in I} s_i \to s
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              a
              %em partial
              function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M(\sigma): \prod_{i \in I} M(s_i) \to M(s)
            %p such that
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  For each
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi Σ
                          %mi t
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \in \Sigma_t
                  the function
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi σ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M(\sigma)
                  is a total function;
              %li
                %p
                  For each
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo −
                        %msub
                          %mi Σ
                          %mi t
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \in \Sigma - \Sigma_t
                  of type
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                            %mrow
                              %mi i
                              %mo ∈
                              %mi I
                          %msub
                            %mi s
                            %mi i
                          %mo →
                          %mi s
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{i \in I} s_i \to s
                  = succeed "-tuple" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi I
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mi i
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mrow
                            %mi i
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi I
                        %mo ∈
                        %munder
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                          %mrow
                            %mi i
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi I
                        %mi M
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi s
                          %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = \langle a_i \rangle_{i \in I} \in \prod_{i \in I} M(s_i),
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi σ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M(\sigma)(a)
                  is defined if and only if all the equations in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Def
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi σ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Def(\sigma)
                  are satisfied at the argument
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              %li
                %p
                  All the equations of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                  are satisfied (i.e. the restrictions of both partial functions to the intersection of their domains of definitions are equal).
          %p
            Homomorphisms of models
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                  %mo :
                  %mi M
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta: M \to M'
            are defined in the standard way: a collection of functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta(s): M(s) \to M'(s)
            for each sort of the signature
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            which are compatible with the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M(\sigma), M'(\sigma)
            in the evident way.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p The point is that (in the finitary case) either notion of theory may be used to specify the same category of models, and that
          .standout
            %p
              Categories of models of finitary essentially algebraic theories are precisely equivalent to
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+category"} locally finitely presentable categories
              = succeed "es." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sketch"} sketch
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} duality
            between essentially algebraic theories and their categories of models is given by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel-Ulmer+duality"} Gabriel-Ulmer duality
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A (multisorted)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                is the same thing (has the same models) as a finitary essentially algebraic theory in which all operations are total. If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T
                is the opposite of the category of finitely presented
                = succeed "-algebras," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Lex
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mi T
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Set
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lex(C_T, Set)
            %li
              %p As mentioned above, categories are models of a finitary essentially algebraic theory.
            %li
              %p
                An equational
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Horn+theory"} Horn theory
                is essentially algebraic, but not all essentially algebraic theories are equational Horn theories. Perhaps surprisingly,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
                is not the category of models of any equational Horn theory, nor is even the category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Pos
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pos
                of posets. See
                %a{:href => "ftp://132.206.150.195/pub/barr/pdffiles/horn.pdf"} this paper
                by Barr for a proof. Essentially algebraic theories are equivalent to
                %em partial
                Horn theories (
                %a{:href => "#PalmgrenVickers"}> Palmgren, Vickers
                ).
            %li
              %p
                An equivalent formulation is as a
                = succeed "," do
                  %em cartesian theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric theory
                in which disjunction
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigvee
                is not used, and each use of existential quantification
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∃
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists
                must be accompanied by a proof that existence is unique. See
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elephant"} Elephant
